1
00:00:00,321 --> 00:00:02,651
Last time, we saw chlorine written like this,

2
00:00:02,972 --> 00:00:05,382
with a thirty-five above and a seventeen below.

3
00:00:06,005 --> 00:00:07,570
It's called nuclide notation,

4
00:00:08,093 --> 00:00:10,261
and it's a compact label for what's inside

5
00:00:10,322 --> 00:00:10,703
an atom.

6
00:00:11,346 --> 00:00:12,767
By the end of this video, you'll be

7
00:00:12,808 --> 00:00:14,830
able to read any label like this, and

8
00:00:14,851 --> 00:00:18,195
count exactly how many protons, neutrons and electrons

9
00:00:18,276 --> 00:00:20,738
it describes, for an atom or an ion.

10
00:00:22,143 --> 00:00:24,150
So let's start with the three particles we're

11
00:00:24,191 --> 00:00:24,511
counting.

12
00:00:25,556 --> 00:00:28,343
Every atom has a tiny, dense centre called

13
00:00:28,364 --> 00:00:28,944
the nucleus.

14
00:00:29,547 --> 00:00:31,974
It holds protons, which each carry a charge

15
00:00:32,055 --> 00:00:35,224
of plus one, and neutrons, which carry no

16
00:00:35,286 --> 00:00:36,007
charge at all.

17
00:00:36,751 --> 00:00:39,079
Protons and neutrons have almost the same mass,

18
00:00:39,422 --> 00:00:41,128
so we give each a relative mass of

19
00:00:41,370 --> 00:00:41,489
one.

20
00:00:42,678 --> 00:00:44,450
Around the nucleus are the electrons.

21
00:00:44,893 --> 00:00:47,116
Each has a charge of minus one, but

22
00:00:47,217 --> 00:00:48,959
its mass is less than a thousandth of

23
00:00:49,001 --> 00:00:51,163
a proton's, so we count it as almost

24
00:00:51,245 --> 00:00:51,584
zero.

25
00:00:52,510 --> 00:00:54,426
This drawing is a model, not to scale.

26
00:00:54,889 --> 00:00:57,301
A real nucleus is far smaller, compared with

27
00:00:57,322 --> 00:00:57,904
the whole atom.

28
00:00:59,284 --> 00:01:00,190
Now the bottom number.

29
00:01:00,633 --> 00:01:02,767
Seventeen is the atomic number, written Z.

30
00:01:03,753 --> 00:01:05,720
It's the number of protons in the nucleus.

31
00:01:06,747 --> 00:01:08,980
And the number of protons decides which element

32
00:01:09,081 --> 00:01:09,322
it is.

33
00:01:10,047 --> 00:01:12,762
Every atom with seventeen protons is chlorine.

34
00:01:13,266 --> 00:01:15,036
Give it one more proton, and it isn't

35
00:01:15,077 --> 00:01:16,625
chlorine any more, it's argon.

36
00:01:17,310 --> 00:01:19,335
That's also why the periodic table runs in

37
00:01:19,496 --> 00:01:20,539
order of atomic number.

38
00:01:21,102 --> 00:01:23,588
Chlorine is element seventeen, so it sits in

39
00:01:23,629 --> 00:01:24,651
the seventeenth box.

40
00:01:25,261 --> 00:01:28,409
The top number, thirty-five, is the mass number,

41
00:01:28,770 --> 00:01:29,111
written A.

42
00:01:30,134 --> 00:01:33,027
It counts every particle in the nucleus, protons

43
00:01:33,088 --> 00:01:33,832
plus neutrons.

44
00:01:34,535 --> 00:01:36,963
Electrons aren't counted, because they add almost no

45
00:01:37,024 --> 00:01:37,304
mass.

46
00:01:37,967 --> 00:01:40,233
So to find the neutrons, take the mass

47
00:01:40,274 --> 00:01:42,499
number and subtract the atomic number.

48
00:01:42,982 --> 00:01:46,071
Thirty-five minus seventeen gives eighteen neutrons.

49
00:01:46,977 --> 00:01:47,964
What about electrons?

50
00:01:48,767 --> 00:01:50,973
In a neutral atom, the plus and minus

51
00:01:50,994 --> 00:01:53,922
charges cancel, so the number of electrons equals

52
00:01:53,943 --> 00:01:54,825
the number of protons.

53
00:01:55,468 --> 00:01:57,965
This chlorine atom has seventeen electrons.

54
00:01:58,711 --> 00:02:01,520
But in salt, chlorine isn't a neutral atom.

55
00:02:02,063 --> 00:02:04,628
It has gained one extra electron, so it

56
00:02:04,669 --> 00:02:06,352
carries a charge of minus one.

57
00:02:07,035 --> 00:02:09,299
That makes it an ion, a charged particle,

58
00:02:09,661 --> 00:02:11,584
written with a small minus sign at the

59
00:02:11,625 --> 00:02:12,085
top right.

60
00:02:12,928 --> 00:02:16,377
The chloride ion has seventeen protons and eighteen

61
00:02:16,478 --> 00:02:16,918
electrons.

62
00:02:17,766 --> 00:02:18,876
Notice what didn't change.

63
00:02:19,422 --> 00:02:22,410
The protons, and so the element, stayed exactly

64
00:02:22,451 --> 00:02:22,771
the same.

65
00:02:23,601 --> 00:02:26,807
Let's try iron, with fifty-six on top, twenty-six

66
00:02:26,867 --> 00:02:29,152
below, and two plus at the top right.

67
00:02:30,037 --> 00:02:32,611
Protons: that's the atomic number, twenty-six.

68
00:02:33,356 --> 00:02:36,433
Neutrons: fifty-six minus twenty-six, which is thirty.

69
00:02:37,196 --> 00:02:37,657
Electrons:

70
00:02:38,279 --> 00:02:41,206
a neutral iron atom would have twenty-six, but

71
00:02:41,327 --> 00:02:43,933
two plus means it has lost two, so

72
00:02:43,994 --> 00:02:44,776
it has twenty-four.

73
00:02:45,458 --> 00:02:48,603
Check the charge: twenty-six positive protons and twenty-four

74
00:02:48,724 --> 00:02:50,707
negative electrons leave plus two.

75
00:02:51,354 --> 00:02:51,760
It matches.

76
00:02:52,610 --> 00:02:53,173
Quick prediction.

77
00:02:53,738 --> 00:02:57,228
Here's another kind of chlorine atom, chlorine-thirty-seven.

78
00:02:58,253 --> 00:02:59,238
Is it still chlorine?

79
00:03:00,083 --> 00:03:03,701
And what's different inside it, compared with chlorine-thirty-five?

80
00:03:04,648 --> 00:03:05,091
Have a think.

81
00:03:09,195 --> 00:03:11,961
Yes, it's still chlorine, because it still has

82
00:03:12,122 --> 00:03:13,224
seventeen protons.

83
00:03:14,008 --> 00:03:15,503
The difference is in the neutrons.

84
00:03:16,148 --> 00:03:18,856
Thirty-seven minus seventeen gives twenty,

85
00:03:19,519 --> 00:03:22,146
two more than chlorine-thirty-five's eighteen.

86
00:03:23,391 --> 00:03:25,519
Atoms of the same element with different numbers

87
00:03:25,560 --> 00:03:27,688
of neutrons are called isotopes.

88
00:03:28,532 --> 00:03:31,356
And because both have seventeen electrons, arranged the

89
00:03:31,397 --> 00:03:34,241
same way, they react in essentially the same

90
00:03:34,282 --> 00:03:34,462
way.

91
00:03:35,285 --> 00:03:38,601
Chemistry is mostly about electrons, and neutrons hardly

92
00:03:38,642 --> 00:03:38,983
change it.

93
00:03:39,948 --> 00:03:41,974
So each particle has its own job,

94
00:03:42,476 --> 00:03:43,619
and here's a line to hold onto.

95
00:03:44,445 --> 00:03:45,837
Protons pick the element.

96
00:03:46,585 --> 00:03:48,058
Neutrons pick the isotope.

97
00:03:48,745 --> 00:03:50,292
Electrons pick the charge.

98
00:03:51,238 --> 00:03:52,965
Change the protons, and you've got a different

99
00:03:53,027 --> 00:03:53,287
element.

100
00:03:53,991 --> 00:03:55,937
Change the neutrons, and you've got a different

101
00:03:56,119 --> 00:03:56,539
isotope.

102
00:03:57,324 --> 00:03:59,679
Change the electrons, and you've got an ion.

103
00:04:02,869 --> 00:04:04,976
Isotopes can also differ in another way.

104
00:04:05,679 --> 00:04:09,685
Carbon-twelve and carbon-thirteen are stable, but carbon-fourteen is

105
00:04:09,927 --> 00:04:11,469
unstable, or radioactive.

106
00:04:12,091 --> 00:04:14,403
Sooner or later, its nucleus breaks down and

107
00:04:14,445 --> 00:04:15,530
gives out radiation.

108
00:04:16,258 --> 00:04:16,461
Why?

109
00:04:17,273 --> 00:04:20,103
Inside a nucleus, the positive protons repel each

110
00:04:20,144 --> 00:04:20,303
other.

111
00:04:20,786 --> 00:04:23,770
A very short-range attraction, called the strong nuclear

112
00:04:23,812 --> 00:04:26,556
force, holds all the protons and neutrons together.

113
00:04:27,341 --> 00:04:30,195
Neutrons add that attraction without adding any repulsion.

114
00:04:30,939 --> 00:04:33,684
Small nuclei are stable with roughly equal numbers

115
00:04:33,726 --> 00:04:36,531
of protons and neutrons, while heavy ones need

116
00:04:36,732 --> 00:04:40,400
extra neutrons, like lead-two-hundred-and-eight, with eighty-two protons and

117
00:04:40,441 --> 00:04:41,783
a hundred and twenty-six neutrons.

118
00:04:42,626 --> 00:04:44,650
With too many or too few neutrons, a

119
00:04:44,691 --> 00:04:46,053
nucleus is unstable.

120
00:04:46,676 --> 00:04:48,160
What it gives out when it breaks down

121
00:04:48,643 --> 00:04:49,465
is for a later video.

122
00:04:50,190 --> 00:04:50,653
Your turn.

123
00:04:51,238 --> 00:04:55,730
Magnesium-twenty-four two plus and magnesium-twenty-five two plus both

124
00:04:55,811 --> 00:04:57,314
have atomic number twelve.

125
00:04:57,997 --> 00:05:00,603
State the number of protons, neutrons and electrons

126
00:05:00,644 --> 00:05:03,070
in each, and identify how the two particles

127
00:05:03,252 --> 00:05:03,772
are related.

128
00:05:04,578 --> 00:05:05,504
Pause here to answer.

129
00:05:16,114 --> 00:05:18,635
Both have twelve protons, since that's the atomic

130
00:05:18,696 --> 00:05:21,877
number, and both have ten electrons, because two

131
00:05:21,918 --> 00:05:23,558
plus means two electrons were lost.

132
00:05:24,301 --> 00:05:25,287
The neutrons differ.

133
00:05:25,831 --> 00:05:28,899
Twenty-four minus twelve is twelve, and twenty-five minus

134
00:05:28,920 --> 00:05:29,841
twelve is thirteen.

135
00:05:30,825 --> 00:05:33,510
Same element with different neutrons, so they're isotopes,

136
00:05:34,253 --> 00:05:36,437
and both are ions with the same charge.

137
00:05:37,201 --> 00:05:39,148
Fourteen electrons is the classic slip.

138
00:05:39,732 --> 00:05:42,249
A positive charge means electrons were lost, not

139
00:05:42,290 --> 00:05:42,551
gained.

140
00:05:44,288 --> 00:05:44,758
Last one.

141
00:05:45,542 --> 00:05:49,147
A particle has sixteen protons, eighteen neutrons and

142
00:05:49,308 --> 00:05:50,369
eighteen electrons.

143
00:05:51,030 --> 00:05:54,242
Write its nuclide notation, including its charge, and

144
00:05:54,303 --> 00:05:55,025
name the element.

145
00:05:55,769 --> 00:05:56,736
Pause here to answer.

146
00:06:07,629 --> 00:06:10,305
Sixteen protons makes it element sixteen, sulfur.

147
00:06:10,950 --> 00:06:13,458
The mass number is sixteen plus eighteen, thirty-four.

148
00:06:14,343 --> 00:06:16,468
It has two more electrons than protons, so

149
00:06:16,530 --> 00:06:17,773
its charge is two minus.

150
00:06:18,295 --> 00:06:19,597
So it's sulfur-thirty-four,

151
00:06:20,120 --> 00:06:22,725
with thirty-four on top, sixteen below, and two

152
00:06:22,766 --> 00:06:23,828
minus at the top right.

153
00:06:24,631 --> 00:06:26,559
Writing fifty-two on top is the slip.

154
00:06:27,082 --> 00:06:29,598
The mass number counts the nucleus only, never

155
00:06:29,619 --> 00:06:30,222
the electrons.

156
00:06:31,928 --> 00:06:33,693
So a mass number is always a whole

157
00:06:33,754 --> 00:06:35,639
number, because it counts whole particles.

158
00:06:36,622 --> 00:06:38,647
But look up chlorine on the periodic table,

159
00:06:38,948 --> 00:06:41,414
and its mass is listed as thirty-five point

160
00:06:41,515 --> 00:06:42,136
four five.

161
00:06:42,839 --> 00:06:44,684
Not thirty-five, not thirty-seven,

162
00:06:45,207 --> 00:06:46,289
and not a whole number at all.

163
00:06:47,319 --> 00:06:48,391
Next time, we'll see why.

164
00:06:48,935 --> 00:06:51,483
It's an average over both isotopes, weighted by

165
00:06:51,544 --> 00:06:52,707
how common each one is.

166
00:06:55,461 --> 00:06:56,284
Here are the key notes.

167
00:06:56,807 --> 00:06:58,355
Pause the video if you need more time

168
00:06:58,395 --> 00:06:58,817
to copy them.
